@Tim Hm, well it seems like neither "their" nor "cruciverbalists'" are unambiguously adjectives, because they swallow the article. I might say, "I have his book" or "I have Joyce's book" but not "I have incomprehensible book," since the latter construction misses an article.

In spite of this, you could reasonably decide to call the pronominal determiners "possessive adjectives" (and many do). But similarly, in spite of the fact that they do not stand alone, you could also decide to call them "dependent possessive pronouns" (and many do). If I wanted to be very careful, I might not use either term.

As is so often the case, it is a question of terminology, albeit an interesting one. But since there are certainly many credible people who do classify "their" as a pronoun, I think it is a fair clue. After all, when a word can be spelled in multiple ways, the variants are acceptable in a crossword (cf. SHEIKS), so why not extend the same principle to grammar?

